CAPSTONE.push(...[{"question":"A court finds a state rule conflicts with a valid federal statute. Which principle is implicated?","options":["Federal supremacy","Attorney-client privilege","Venue","Consideration"],"answer":0,"why":"Valid federal law may preempt conflicting state requirements."},{"question":"A lawyer predicts success solely from a case decided in a different jurisdiction. What is missing?","options":["Verification of controlling local authority","More emphatic wording","A longer introduction","An oral promise"],"answer":0,"why":"Persuasive precedent must be distinguished from binding authority."},{"question":"A statute covers only defined sellers and expressly excludes certain transactions. What is the correct method?","options":["Analyze defined terms and exclusions","Read only its heading","Assume universal applicability","Ignore legislative text"],"answer":0,"why":"The legal elements include definitions and exceptions."},{"question":"A legal memorandum reports a contested witness statement as a proven fact. Which correction helps most?","options":["Identify it as disputed testimony and test corroboration","Delete the opposing party","Invent additional evidence","Promise a favorable result"],"answer":0,"why":"Facts, allegations and inferences should be separated."},{"question":"A police officer speaks with someone on a sidewalk. The student says Miranda warnings are always required. What is the issue?","options":["Warnings generally depend on custodial interrogation","All police discussions are trials","Warnings apply only to contract claims","Every sidewalk encounter is custody"],"answer":0,"why":"Custody and interrogation require context-specific analysis."},{"question":"A lawyer represents adverse parties in the same litigation. What is the best first analysis?","options":["Evaluate applicable conflict rules, including consentability","Obtain any signature and ignore the rest","Keep both clients unaware","Assume there is no conflict"],"answer":0,"why":"Some conflicts are not consentable and professional rules govern."},{"question":"A civil plaintiff meets the preponderance standard but not beyond reasonable doubt. Which statement is generally correct?","options":["The ordinary civil burden may still be met","Criminal conviction automatically follows","Every civil matter requires criminal proof","No civil suit can proceed"],"answer":0,"why":"Ordinary civil and criminal cases use different standards."},{"question":"The only source given is a summary claiming every state has identical landlord deadlines. What is appropriate?","options":["Check current statutes for the relevant state","Use the summary as a binding statute","Presume all state laws are uniform","Treat social posts as appellate precedent"],"answer":0,"why":"State laws vary, and primary law must be verified."},{"question":"An appellate brief asks judges to retry all witnesses without identifying an error. What's the weakness?","options":["Appellate review is generally record- and standard-bound","Witness testimony is always inadmissible","Appeals cannot consider law","Trial courts never decide facts"],"answer":0,"why":"Appeals generally review lower court decisions rather than rehear entire trials."},{"question":"A case has been reversed on appeal after a student found it online. Why does it matter?","options":["Later history may defeat its authority","All old cases are invalid","Case citations never change","Only news reports count"],"answer":0,"why":"Researchers must validate subsequent treatment and authority."},{"question":"A client sends funds intended to be held by counsel. What professional duty is especially implicated?","options":["Safekeeping and accounting of client property","Jury instructions","Personal jurisdiction","Criminal charging"],"answer":0,"why":"Rules usually require proper separation and recordkeeping for client property."},{"question":"A case involves disputed causation and a possible comparative fault defense. Which analysis is best?","options":["Address each relevant element and both sides' evidence","Declare breach automatically proves injury","Avoid factual detail","Apply a single state's law to all states"],"answer":0,"why":"A complete tort analysis considers elements, defenses and governing jurisdiction."}]);
CAPSTONE.push(...[{"question":"A trial court dismissed a complaint for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. The claimant says a sympathetic jury should decide anyway.","options":["Jurisdiction is a threshold limit on adjudicative power","Sympathy overrides jurisdiction","Any federal court can hear any dispute"],"answer":0,"why":"A court must have legally conferred subject-matter jurisdiction."},{"question":"A plaintiff cites an editorial headnote as the sole authority for a legal holding.","options":["Check the actual controlling opinion and relevant passage","A headnote is binding text","The issue is automatically waived"],"answer":0,"why":"Headnotes are not a substitute for a court's ruling."},{"question":"A government employee denies a permit due to an applicant's political viewpoint.","options":["Analyze government action and viewpoint discrimination","Treat it as a purely private choice","Assume no constitutional question exists"],"answer":0,"why":"Viewpoint-based government restrictions can create significant First Amendment issues."},{"question":"A statute contains a defined exception that the opposing brief ignores.","options":["Test the statutory elements, definitions and exception","Read only the title","Treat exceptions as unenforceable"],"answer":0,"why":"Interpretation requires consideration of the operative statutory language."},{"question":"A lawyer is unfamiliar with a technical case but assures the client no preparation is needed.","options":["Evaluate competence, preparation and appropriate assistance","Competence cannot be developed","No client communication is necessary"],"answer":0,"why":"Lawyers need sufficient competent preparation and may seek qualified help."},{"question":"A consumer claims a universal warranty right based solely on an advertisement from a different state.","options":["Identify actual promises, governing law and material facts","Assume the other state's rules bind everywhere","Ignore transaction records"],"answer":0,"why":"Warranty and consumer remedies vary with the applicable laws and facts."},{"question":"A party appeals a factual verdict without explaining the standard of review.","options":["Identify the preserved issues and proper standard of review","Ask appellate judges to freely replace all facts","Assume appeal is a second trial"],"answer":0,"why":"Appellate review is constrained by standards, records and procedural preservation."},{"question":"A writer asserts a case is still binding although the relevant law has been amended.","options":["Verify subsequent law and treatment","Keep the old claim because it sounds persuasive","Replace the citation with a blog"],"answer":0,"why":"Changes in law and later decisions can alter precedential authority."},{"question":"A lawyer learns a draft brief quotes a nonexistent case.","options":["Verify and remove false citations, comply with candor duties","File it if it supports the client","Hide it from the client and judge"],"answer":0,"why":"Lawyers must verify citations and abide by duties of candor."},{"question":"A tenant disputes a deposit return but the analyst knows neither the state nor the lease.","options":["Identify jurisdiction, documents, deadlines and statutory exceptions","Apply an arbitrary national rule","Guarantee reimbursement"],"answer":0,"why":"Legal analysis requires the governing state rules and the contract's facts."},{"question":"A city enforces a neutral-looking rule only against one disfavored speaker.","options":["Examine the evidence of selective enforcement and viewpoint bias","Assume a written neutral label resolves the case","Ignore comparable speakers"],"answer":0,"why":"Application of a rule may reveal discrimination even where text appears neutral."},{"question":"A plaintiff alleges a fall but provides no evidence of injury or causation.","options":["Evaluate each liability element and available proof","Assume notice proves every element","Treat the complaint as a judgment"],"answer":0,"why":"A negligence claim requires distinct elements, including causation and damages."}]);
